{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>On the second day Netanel son of Zuar, prince of Issachar, offered.</b> הקריב is spelled deficient (without a yod). And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: why is it said of all the princes קרבנו (his offering), while here it says הקריב את קרבנו (he offered his offering)? Because he offered by divine word: for the other tribes sought to offer, as they were greater than he, and they were overruled from heaven, and the tribe of Issachar was commanded to draw near to the altar's offering and to offer his offering. You may know that it is so written הקריב deficient (without a yod), for he was far off and was brought near to come — all this, why? Because they were knowing in Torah; therefore it is written (I Chronicles 12:33): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים לדעת מה יעשה ישראל ראשיהם מאתים וכל אחיהם על פיהם״ — this teaches you that all of them concur with the halakhah according to their word."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:39.919163157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}